<SubSection title="Radiative Heat Transfer" id="Radiative Heat Transfer">
    <p> Radiative heat transfer is the transport of thermal energy emitted as electromagnetic waves. It is attributed to changes in electron configuration within the atoms of the target matter. </p>

    <p> No medium (i.e. physical contact, fluid motion) is required in this mode of heat transfer, as it is spontaneously emitted. </p>
    
    <p> The maximum flux at which radiation may be <i>emitted</i> by a body is governed by the <b> Stefan-Boltzmann Law </b>: </p> 
    
    <DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{q}{A} = q^{''} = O T^4" title="Stefan-Boltzmann Law of Radiative Heat Transfer" />
    
    <p> Let's go over the variables present in these equations: </p>
    
    <Itemize>
    <Item><InlineEquation equation="q (W)" /> = rate of heat emission </Item>
    <Item><InlineEquation equation="A (m^2)" /> = area of radiating surface</Item>
    <Item><InlineEquation equation="q^{''}_x (\\frac{W}{m^2})" /> = rate of heat flow per unit area</Item>
    <Item><InlineEquation equation="\sigma = 5.67*10^-8 \\frac{W}{m^2k^4}" /> = Stefan-Boltzmann constant</Item>
    <Item><InlineEquation equation="T (C or K)" /> = temperature of the radiating surface</Item>
    </Itemize>
    
    To calculate the <i>received</i> energy from surroundings, the net exchange can be calculated as such:
    
    <DisplayEquation equation="\\q^{''}_{1-surroundings} = \sigma(T_{1}^{4}-T_{surroundings}^{4})" title="Stefan-Boltzmann Law of Radiative Heat Transfer" /> 
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Governing Equations & Boundary Conditions of Heat Transfer" id="Governing Equations & Boundary Conditions of Heat Transfer">
 <p> In order to predict the temperature distributions in a system, we need two things: a governing equation and a set of boundary and initial conditions.  
</p> 

<p> The governing heat equation is derived from an energy balance applied to a differential control volume and encapsulates the combined effects of energy storage,
conduction, convection, and internal heat generation. However, to obtain a solution, we must utilize known quantities called boundary conditions that describe heat 
transfer at particular surfaces and interfaces, as well as initial conditions for time-dependent problems.
</p> 

<p>
In the following sections, we introduce the general heat transfer equation and the most common types of boundary conditions in heat transfer problems. 
</p> 

</SubSection>

<SubSection title="General Heat Transfer Equation" id="General Heat Transfer Equation">

<h4><strong>Governing Equation of Heat Transfer</strong></h4>
<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{\\partial T}{\\partial t} + u\\frac{\\partial T}{\\partial x} = \\frac{k}{\\rho c_p}\\frac{\\partial^2 T}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{Q}{\\rho c_p}" title="Governing Heat Equation (Cartesian Coordinates)" background="True"/>

<p>
The governing heat equation shown above is the general one-dimensional form used in this course.
It is written in Cartesian coordinates and includes all possible mechanisms that can contribute to heat
transfer in a system.
</p>

<p>
In practice, many problems involve simplifying assumptions (e.g., steady state, no fluid flow, or no internal
heat generation), which allow certain terms to be removed. 
</p>

<p>
The meaning of each term and the conditions under which it should be retained or dropped are summarized below in Figure 1.
</p>

<Image src="/transfer/Governing_Equation_Tables.png">
  <span class="fw-bold">Figure 1</span>: Table summarizes the physical meaning of each term and 
when it should be retained or removed based on problem conditions.
</Image>

<p>
Figure 2 displays all variations of the governing equation that might be obtained after simplifying it using system conditions. 
</p>

<Image src="/transfer/Governing_Equation_Variations.png">
  <span class="fw-bold">Figure 2</span>: Table summarizes the possible variations of the governing equations depending on which terms are retained or
removed based on problem conditions. Table adapted from Datta (2017): Chapter 3 (Table 3.2).
</Image>

</SubSection>

